1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,400
ECHO: Hi! I’m Echo. I turn Earth’s data into music.

2
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:15,200
ECHO: I have a city to watch, some sounds to teach you, and one important position to fill.

3
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,400
ECHO: Your mission? Protect Dhaka. Your first tool? Your ears.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
ECHO: Ready to join me?

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,600
PRESENTER: We are ByteAro, and our idea is Earth Jukebox: Guardian of Dhaka.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,900
PRESENTER: This nine-to-ten-minute game combines NASA environmental data, music, and decisions.

7
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:42,300
PRESENTER: Players learn how to recognize changing conditions and use that knowledge to protect a virtual city.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,600
ECHO: First, tell me your name. I’ll save it for your boarding pass!

9
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:55,400
ECHO: Drag across the city to explore. Listen to the warning. Tap to place a fix.

10
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,400
ECHO: Let’s practice in the Sound Lab.

11
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,400
ECHO: This melody represents heat. Hear the notes getting higher?

12
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,800
ECHO: The drums represent rainfall,

13
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,400
ECHO: and these soft chords represent trees and green spaces.

14
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:24,600
ECHO: Try changing each slider. Make a heat wave, increase the rain, then bring the conditions back to normal.

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
PRESENTER: Sound changes appear alongside visual cues and labels. A five-sound ears test checks what players have learned.

16
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,700
ECHO: Missed one? Listen again. I’ll give you a hint.

17
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:41,400
ECHO: Welcome to your city, Guardian. Our journey takes us from 2000 to 2050.

18
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,000
ECHO: Listen—there’s a heat warning!

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
ECHO: You found it! Now you have twenty seconds and a limited budget.

20
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,400
PRESENTER: Players choose among trees, reflective roofs, water spaces, and cooling centers. Different measures serve different purposes, so spending wisely matters.

21
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:17,300
ECHO: Let’s review. Which areas did you protect? Where do people still need help?

22
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:21,900
PRESENTER: After each round, Echo explains the result and the underlying data.

23
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,600
PRESENTER: If more than half the city is unprotected, players retry.

24
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:29,900
PRESENTER: As the city expands, space becomes another resource they must manage.

25
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:34,000
ECHO: My music has a source. Here’s where the numbers come in.

26
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:42,500
PRESENTER: We plan to build earlier rounds around NASA historical data, with suitable climate projections supporting the 2035 and 2050 scenarios.

27
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
PRESENTER: Our team designs how values become sound and gameplay.

28
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:52,900
PRESENTER: Coin costs and protection effects are simplified assumptions, clearly distinguished from the data.

29
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:57,300
ECHO: The future rounds show possibilities—not promises.

30
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:09,700
ECHO: One final decision, Guardian. Which path will your city explore?

31
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:15,100
PRESENTER: Choices about energy and green space lead to contrasting future scenarios within the game.

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,300
PRESENTER: Then players receive a boarding pass with their name, score, future and achievement badges.

33
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,900
PRESENTER: A QR code lets them invite others to play.

34
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:29,500
ECHO: Want to try a different strategy? Your next mission is waiting.

35
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:42,700
PRESENTER: Our goal is to help players recognize environmental patterns, consider limited resources, and understand the decisions they make.

36
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,900
ECHO: In Earth Jukebox, you can’t win unless you learn.

37
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:49,900
ECHO: I’m Echo. You’re the guardian.

38
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,700
ECHO: Let’s listen to Earth together.
